  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe upper low that has been slowly moving across the High Plains \nwill be in the process of weakening and filling Monday. Still \nappears that there will be a chance for at least some scattered \nshowers across the area. At the surface, strong surface high \npressure should be located across southeast Canada. This will back a \ncooler Canadian airmass into the region. The coolest weather will be \nMonday into Tuesday, with expected cloud cover Monday helping to \nhold temperatures down. In fact, highs Monday may struggle out of \nthe upper 50s across portions of north central Nebraska. Ridging \naloft will then translate eastward across the region Tuesday. This \nshould help to bring at least a brief pause in rain chances. It \nremains cooler than average as the low-level flow will be \noriginating from the high pressure across southeast Canada. Still \nrather cool Wednesday into Thursday despite winds shifting to the \nsouth-southeast. The Canadian airmass will be recycled back \nnorthward during this time, so no big warm up is in the offing.\nA quick moving shortwave then appears it will cross the area \nWednesday through Thursday morning. This would be the next \nchance for rainfall as it crosses the area.",
